Z||000000||Fearless Freep||10-07-2000||02:39 PM||||This belt pulley replacement is turning into a big pain in the ***  I cant seem to get that pulley bolt loose (the big one in the center).  Any sugestions, other than emergency brake on and in gear.||||reg||1
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||10-07-2000||03:18 PM||||I have replace two of them years ago. I think that the secret is that the 15 mm bolt is reversed threaded. Lefty loosey, righty tighty is reversed. Turn the bolt to the left and the pulley should come off. <P>Hope that helps. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fearless Freep||10-07-2000||04:13 PM||||If its reversed then it would be righty loosey and lefty tighty.....??||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||10-07-2000||05:54 PM||||Righty.<P>Now I'm curious... what pulley are you guys talking about??  If it's the crank pulley on a Fiero V6, its center bolt has right hand (conventional)threads...     ||||reg||5
Z||000004||Fearless Freep||10-07-2000||06:05 PM||||Stu...yeah was trying to install one of those friggin power pulleys.  Still cant get the bolt to losen.  Going to stick new belts on tomorrow until I can take it by Marty (the mechanic) and his assortment of impact tools.<P>But the biggest thing is getting belts on it. The old ones damn near fell apart in my hands when I started looking at them.  Also noticed my rubber bushings have gone to pot as well.<P>Amazing what you find when the tire is off.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||10-07-2000||06:43 PM||||Okay, I guess you mean "yes, it's the crank pulley on a V6."  If so, here's how I got mine off (to R&R the timing chain)...<P>Remove (at least) one spark plug.  Turn motor until that cylinder is on its compression stroke, and roughly at bottom dead center (verify by probing spark plug hole with screwdriver or whatever).  <P>Get an old discarded, but not crumbling, appliance power cord.  Thread the appliance cord (not the plug end)into the cylinder - it won't fall in, because the plug will stop it.  Now turn motor CCW (lefty loosey) with your socket, until the engine locks up.  It locks due to the hydrocordostatic principle -(a well-known scientific law I just made up) which states that liquids and old power cords are virtually incompressible.<P>I put a six-point socket on the crank pulley, along with pretty much all of the extensions I owned, then stuck a breaker bar on the end of all that.  The breaker bar handle was thus outboard of the wheelwell.  I supported the head of the breaker bar on a jackstand, and put a 24" long pipe on the handle, so that the pipe pointed aft.  Got the picture?<P>Press inward on the head of the breaker bar, to make sure the socket is all the way on the center bolt of the crank, then apply a little (lefty) torque to make it stay on there.  Maintain the torque while you reposition the head of the wrench on the jackstand.  Now you gradually apply more CCW torque to the pipe.  Remember, the pipe is sticking aft (rearward), so you're pressing <B>down</B> on the end of the pipe.  The jackstand is there to react the down force, so that the socket sees (almost) pure CCW torque.<P>If the jackstand weren't there, the whole mess would just swing down and come off the crank pulley bolt (and possibly round off a few corners).  You'll start leaning on the pipe; pretty soon you're standing on it, and finally you hear a "tink-!," which means the bolt decided to loosen.  <P>That's how I did it - hope it works for you, too   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !                <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 10-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Scott_M||10-07-2000||08:00 PM||||Also, make sure you check out Ogre's article in the cave, under serpintine belts. It's a big help. Was for me anyway. Good luck. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||10-09-2000||02:02 AM||||Make sure the cylinder you choose is not on the exhaust stroke if you use Stu's method!  You could bend your exhaust valve.<P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">      <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||StuGood||10-09-2000||11:18 AM||||Good point, Standard!  Didn't think of that.  I edited the above write-up.  Thanks for setting me straight  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000009||bHooper||10-09-2000||10:00 PM||||just wait until you have access to the impact wrench!  that way it is simple and painless!<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||10
